One common way to introduce vulnerabilities is through **Insecure Deserialization**. When an application uses `ObjectInputStream` to deserialize untrusted data from a user, an attacker can craft a malicious serialized object to execute arbitrary code on the server (Remote Code Execution).

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.util.Base64;

public class DeserializationVulnerability {
    // This method is vulnerable because it attempts to deserialize 
    // an object directly from a string provided by the user.
    public void processUserObject(String userInput) throws Exception {
        byte[] data = Base64.getDecoder().decode(userInput);
        ByteArrayInputStream bais = new ByteArrayInputStream(data);
        ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(bais);

        // Use of ObjectInputStream on untrusted input is vulnerable to RCE
        Object obj = ois.readObject(); 
        System.out.println("Received: " + obj.toString());
    }
}
***

import java.io.*;
import java.util.Base64;

public class DeserializationFix {
    // To fix this, we avoid using standard Java serialization for untrusted data.
    // Instead, we use a data-only format like JSON with a safe library.
    public void processUserObject(String jsonInput) throws Exception {
        // Using a library like Jackson or Gson to parse JSON is safe
        // because they do not execute logic during the reconstruction 
        // of the data objects, unlike ObjectInputStream.
        com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.ObjectMapper mapper = new com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.ObjectMapper();

        // Mapping input to a specific, predefined Data Transfer Object (DTO) 
        // prevents an attacker from instancing arbitrary gadget classes.
        UserDTO user = mapper.readValue(jsonInput, UserDTO.class);

        System.out.println("Received User: " + user.getName());
    }
}

// Simple DTO to control the data structure
class UserDTO implements Serializable {
    private String name;
    public String getName() { return name; }
    public void setName(String name) { this.name = name; }
}
```

